  {
   "slug": "quarterly-estimated-taxes-for-freelancers",
   "title": "Quarterly estimated taxes: a calm guide for freelancers",
   "date": "2026-10-11T09:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "tax"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "freelancers",
    "deadlines"
   ],
   "featured_media": "calendar",
   "excerpt": "No employer is withholding tax for you, so you pay it four times a year. A simple system takes the stress out of it.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>When you work for yourself, nobody withholds tax from your income. Instead you pay estimated tax four times a year. Miss a payment and you face penalties; overpay and you lend the government money for free.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">A simple system</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Open a separate savings account just for tax.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Move 25–30% of every payment you receive into it the same day.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pay each quarterly estimate from that account.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Adjust the percentage after your first year's return.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The four dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Payment</th><th>Covers income from</th><th>Due</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Q1</td><td>January–March</td><td>April 15</td></tr><tr><td>Q2</td><td>April–May</td><td>June 15</td></tr><tr><td>Q3</td><td>June–August</td><td>September 15</td></tr><tr><td>Q4</td><td>September–December</td><td>January 15</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The savings account is the whole trick. Once the money is set aside, the deadline is just a transfer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Senior tax adviser</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In your first year, the safe-harbour rule usually lets you base payments on last year's tax, which avoids penalties even if you earn more.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Mei L.",
     "email": "mei@example.com",
     "content": "Separate account changed my life. No more panic in April.",
     "date": "2026-10-12T21:00:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "bookkeeping-habits-that-save-hours",
   "title": "Five bookkeeping habits that save hours at year end",
   "date": "2026-09-30T08:30:00",
   "categories": [
    "bookkeeping",
    "small-business"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "records"
   ],
   "featured_media": "receipts",
   "excerpt": "Fifteen minutes a week now is worth two days in March. These habits keep your books ready for tax season.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Most year-end stress comes from twelve months of small things left undone. A short weekly routine keeps the books current and makes tax season almost boring.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Snap receipts</strong> the moment you get them.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Reconcile weekly</strong>, not monthly.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Separate accounts</strong> for business and personal spending.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Invoice on the day</strong> the work is finished.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Review one report</strong> every month: profit and cash, side by side.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Which software?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The best bookkeeping software is the one you will actually open. All the common packages are good enough; we work with all of them and can help you choose.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If your books are already behind, our catch-up service brings them up to date in a few weeks.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "hiring-your-first-employee",
   "title": "Hiring your first employee: the payroll checklist",
   "date": "2026-09-17T10:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "payroll",
    "small-business"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "employers"
   ],
   "featured_media": "payroll",
   "excerpt": "Your first hire is exciting and a little intimidating. Here is the paperwork, in the order you need it.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Taking on your first employee is a sign that the business is growing. It also brings a set of new obligations. None of them is difficult, but they need to be done in the right order.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Before the first day</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"ticks\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list ticks\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Register as an employer with the tax office and your state.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Set up workers' compensation insurance.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Collect the employee's tax and eligibility forms.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Every pay day</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"ticks\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list ticks\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Calculate gross pay, withholding and employer taxes.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pay the employee and keep a pay record.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Deposit withheld taxes on the required schedule.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Every quarter and year end</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>File quarterly payroll returns and, in January, give each employee their annual wage statement. Our payroll service handles all of this for a flat fee per employee.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Get payroll right from the first pay check. Fixing it later always costs more.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Payroll manager</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "vat-and-sales-tax-for-online-sellers",
   "title": "Sales tax for online sellers: what changed this year",
   "date": "2026-09-03T09:15:00",
   "categories": [
    "tax",
    "small-business"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "online-sales"
   ],
   "featured_media": "invoice",
   "excerpt": "Selling across state lines can create obligations even without a physical presence. Here is how to check where you need to register.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you sell online, you may owe sales tax in states where you have no office, warehouse or employee. Most states now use an economic threshold, based on sales or number of transactions, to decide.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to check</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Export a report of sales by destination state for the last twelve months.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Compare each state's total to its threshold.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Register in any state where you exceed it, before you collect tax there.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Marketplaces often collect and remit tax for sales made through them, but sales from your own website are your responsibility.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"tip-box\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group tip-box\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Good to know:</strong> registering late usually means paying the tax you should have collected out of your own pocket.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "cash-flow-forecast-for-small-business",
   "title": "A cash-flow forecast you will actually keep up to date",
   "date": "2026-08-21T08:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "small-business"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "planning"
   ],
   "featured_media": "growth",
   "excerpt": "Profit is not cash. A simple thirteen-week forecast shows you the tight weeks before they arrive.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Many profitable businesses still run out of cash. Customers pay late, tax bills arrive together, a big order needs stock up front. A cash-flow forecast shows you those weeks in advance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Keep it simple</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"ticks\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list ticks\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>One row per week for the next thirteen weeks.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Money in: expected customer payments, by when they really pay.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Money out: payroll, rent, suppliers, tax and loan payments.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A running balance, with a minimum you never want to go below.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Update it every Monday with the actual numbers from the week before. After a month it takes ten minutes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The forecast is never exactly right. It is right enough to make a phone call to the bank in March instead of June.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Advisory partner</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Carla M.",
     "email": "carla@example.com",
     "content": "We built ours with your template and spotted a crunch in December. Huge help.",
     "date": "2026-08-23T09:00:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "what-to-do-if-you-get-a-tax-letter",
   "title": "What to do if you get a letter from the tax office",
   "date": "2026-08-06T11:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "tax"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "records"
   ],
   "featured_media": "audit",
   "excerpt": "Most letters are routine and easily answered. The key is not to ignore them.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A letter from the tax office can be alarming, but most are routine: a question about a number, a missing form or a small adjustment. The worst thing you can do is put it in a drawer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Read the letter fully and note the response deadline.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Check what it refers to against your return.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Gather any documents that support your figures.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Reply in writing before the deadline, or ask for more time.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the letter mentions an audit or proposes a large change, send us a copy. Clients on any plan get help with tax-office letters at no extra charge.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Keep a copy of everything you send, and note the date and the name of anyone you speak to on the phone.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "year-end-tax-moves",
   "title": "Six year-end tax moves to make before December 31",
   "date": "2026-07-24T09:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "tax",
    "small-business"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "deadlines",
    "planning"
   ],
   "featured_media": "coins",
   "excerpt": "A few decisions made before the year closes can lower this year's bill legally and simply.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Once the year ends, most tax decisions are locked in. A short review in November or December is the best chance to make the most of the rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"ticks\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list ticks\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Top up retirement contributions to the yearly limit.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Buy equipment you need anyway before year end.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Send invoices early, or late, depending on your bracket.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Make charitable gifts and keep the receipts.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Review losses you can use to offset gains.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Check your estimated payments cover this year's income.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Book a review</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every client on the Small business and Growing team plans gets a year-end planning call in November. Personal plan clients can add one for a small fixed fee.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Bring a rough figure for this year's income and any large purchases you are planning. Twenty minutes is usually enough.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  }
 ],
